WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.048
What makes a task risky to delegate.

2
00:00:02.328 --> 00:00:07.128
Agents are improving fast, so a list of “things agents can’t do” ages badly.

3
00:00:07.408 --> 00:00:09.435
Properties of tasks age much better.

4
00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:11.315
Five of them predict trouble.

5
00:00:11.595 --> 00:00:13.109
The outcome can’t be checked.

6
00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:16.355
Everything in this elective rests on verification.

7
00:00:16.635 --> 00:00:24.208
Where there’s no test, no reproducible behavior, no reference output, the agent stops when the work looks done, and so might you.

8
00:00:24.488 --> 00:00:35.261
Data analyses with plausible but wrong joins, numerical code without known answers, and UI behavior nobody has specified all fall here until you create the check.

9
00:00:35.541 --> 00:00:37.931
Mistakes are irreversible or surface late.

10
00:00:38.211 --> 00:00:45.912
Migrations on shared data, deletions, deploys, financial calculations whose errors appear on next month’s invoices.

11
00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:50.416
Checkpoints don’t reach these (lesson 1), and the cost of a miss is high.

12
00:00:50.696 --> 00:00:52.104
The context is tribal.

13
00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:59.829
When the real requirements live in a spreadsheet, a Slack thread, or a senior engineer’s head, the agent fills the gap with a guess.

14
00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:07.427
The best-practices doc’s first diagnosis for bad sessions is exactly this: information it can’t get from the repo.

15
00:01:07.707 --> 00:01:09.584
The task is long and entangled.

16
00:01:09.864 --> 00:01:24.179
METR’s time-horizon research found success falls sharply with task length: models at the time of the study nearly always succeeded on tasks that take experts under about four minutes and rarely on tasks over about four hours,

17
00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:29.771
and the length an agent handles at high reliability is much shorter than at 50%.

18
00:01:30.051 --> 00:01:37.283
Horizons have grown quickly since, but the shape holds: decompose long tasks into checkable pieces (lesson 6).

19
00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:39.163
The point is for you to learn.

20
00:01:39.443 --> 00:01:42.067
If you need to own this code, debug it at 2 a.

21
00:01:42.347 --> 00:01:43.029
m.

22
00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:47.789
, or explain it in a design review, delegating the thinking defeats the purpose.

23
00:01:48.069 --> 00:01:50.843
That’s the subject of the second half of this lesson.

24
00:01:51.123 --> 00:01:56.477
A sixth isn’t a property of the task but of the situation: you can’t evaluate the result.

25
00:01:56.757 --> 00:02:02.048
If you couldn’t tell a correct solution from a plausible wrong one, you’re not delegating, you’re hoping.

26
00:02:02.328 --> 00:02:05.571
Either learn enough to review it, or bring in someone who can.
